-- OAR2: oar2_register_issue001_editors_letter_banner_and_paragraph_publication_v1
-- Corrects dispatch_body for editors_letter_issue001_v1 from an abbreviated placeholder to
-- the full canonical text (matching Assets/Articles/unDrifted/Issue01/registered/
-- undrifted_issue01_editors_letter_article_v1.md verbatim), so this column is a genuine,
-- usable source for eventual Paragraph publication rather than a pointer to another file.

update measures_publication_dispatch
set dispatch_body = E'# From the Editor\n\nThere is a pattern that keeps repeating.\n\nWhen an AI deployment struggles, the first question is often, *What is wrong with the model?*\n\nSometimes the answer is the model.\n\nMore often, it is not.\n\nOver the past several years, I have spent time observing organizations, technologies, workflows, and the increasingly complex systems that connect them. Again and again, I found that the greatest obstacles to successful AI deployment were already present before AI arrived.\n\nFragmented processes, unclear ownership, competing sources of truth, disconnected systems, and operational drift do not disappear when intelligence is added.\n\nThey become more visible.\n\nThat observation became the starting point for this publication.\n\n**unDrifted** exists to explore the relationship between systems, governance, and artificial intelligence. Not through speculation alone, but through operational examples, research questions, and practical methods for measuring the environments AI depends on.\n\nEvery issue will ask difficult questions.\n\nSome articles will examine current events.\n\nSome will explore theory.\n\nOthers will document the development of Measures Registry itself as an evolving reference implementation.\n\nTogether, they form an ongoing investigation into a larger question:\n\n> What would it take to build systems that are genuinely governable?\n\nThe first issue begins with what has become the central observation guiding this work:\n\n**AI isn''t broken. Systems are.**\n\nThat statement is not intended as criticism.\n\nIt is an invitation to look in a different direction.\n\nIf we want trustworthy AI, we have to pay as much attention to the environments surrounding intelligence as we do to intelligence itself.\n\nThank you for taking the time to read the first issue.\n\nI hope it challenges some assumptions, raises better questions, and encourages you to look at your own environment with fresh eyes.\n\nBecause the future of AI will not be shaped by models alone.\n\nIt will be shaped by the systems we choose to build around them.\n\nWelcome to **Issue 001**.\n\nLet''s begin.\n\n**Stephanie Joanne Gaffney**\n*Founder & Systems Designer, Measures Registry*\n*Editor, unDrifted*\n\n---\n\n## Acknowledgment\n\nThis publication was developed through a sustained collaboration between human direction and artificial intelligence.\n\nEvery article, system, and implementation reflects human responsibility for the ideas presented while recognizing the growing role AI can play as a research partner, systems advisor, and implementation collaborator.\n\nMeasures Registry itself is an exploration of that relationship.\n\nThe work presented here would not exist in this form without that collaboration. Human judgment remains responsible for the direction, claims, and decisions. AI assistance helped surface patterns, test language, structure ideas, challenge drift, and accelerate implementation inside a governed process.\n\nThat distinction matters.\n\nBuilding with AI is not the same as delegating to AI.\n\nThe work is strongest when human responsibility and AI capability remain in right relation.\n\n---\n\n## Continue the Conversation\n\nRead the cover story. Explore the dispatches. When you are ready, step beyond the publication and measure your own operational environment through the AI Operations Assessment.\n\nUnderstanding systems is only the first step.\n\nImproving them is the work that follows.'
where dispatch_key = 'editors_letter_issue001_v1';
